// X-macro guard list for superslm_gpu::RunLayerLoopGpu's own host-side guard
// ladder (src/gpu/superslm_gpu.cpp), CPU parity against
// superslm::RunLayerLoopImpl (src/forward/forward_sites.cpp), re-derived
// directly against the current file, not eye-matched against a prior citation.
//
// Three independent hand-counts of forward_sites.cpp's own guard range each
// found only eight guards and each missed the ninth
// (`seq.hidden_codes == nullptr`, forward_sites.cpp:1399), the one guard in
// the function preceded by a 55-line field-by-field struct comment rather
// than a guard-shaped one -- a rule that fails repeatedly by hand-counting has
// not failed because someone was careless: this file is the structural fix
// that closes the class rather than the count.
//
// This is the single, compile-checked enumeration of "how many guards, in
// what order, to what status" the GPU leg's own ladder must implement --
// matching `sequence_layer_state_fields.def`'s own established convention in
// this tree. `GpuLayerLoopGuard::kCount` (superslm_gpu.cpp) is generated from
// this list, so a guard added to CPU's own ladder without a matching row here
// is a COUNT a test can assert against, not a fact resting on a future
// reader's own eye. This file does not restructure CPU's own ladder (out of
// this ticket's own scope, and forward_sites.cpp's own guard shapes are
// heterogeneous enough -- scalar bounds, an overflow-guarded product loop, a
// pointer-nullity check -- that unifying them into one generated predicate
// form is a separate, larger design question) -- it is the metadata table a
// test walks to build one fixture per row and assert CPU and GPU agree,
// status for status, in this exact order.
//
// CORRECTED 2026-08-14: the paragraph above, and the
// two paragraphs before it, describe this file as what closes CPU-guard-
// count drift as a CLASS. It does not, on its own: this file is still a
// hand-written mirror of one reading of `forward_sites.cpp`, `kCount` is
// generated from the mirror, not from CPU, and neither `static_assert`
// comparing `kCount` to a literal `9` -- nor the pin round's own table-walk
// cell, matched to this file's row order by eye -- reads `forward_sites.cpp`
// at all. Falsified by execution: a tenth guard added to both
// `RunLayerLoopImpl` and `RunLayerLoopGpu`, with no matching row here, left
// the build green and the suite unchanged in every count. What actually
// closes the class is `tests/ci/check_gpu_guard_status_parity.py`,
// a build-step check that extracts the real, distinct `SslmForwardStatus`
// set CPU's guard range returns, from `forward_sites.cpp` itself, and
// compares it against this file's own `status_name` column and against
// `RunLayerLoopGpu`'s own ladder -- reddening on exactly the mutation above.
// That check's own honest residual (a tenth CPU guard reusing a status
// already among the nine would not change the derived set, and would not be
// caught) is real and is stated, and proven by execution both ways, in that
// module's own header comment -- not repeated a second time here.
//
// Adding a tenth CPU guard: add one SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD row below, in
// CPU's own source position, AND add the matching `return` in
// RunLayerLoopGpu's own guard ladder (superslm_gpu.cpp) -- the pin round's
// own table-walk cell is what turns a missed row into a red cell rather than
// a silent gap.
//
// SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(enum_name, status_name, cpp_citation)

#ifndef SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD
#error "define SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(enum_name, status_name, cpp_citation) before including this file"
#endif

// Every citation below has been re-derived directly against the current file, more than once,
// as forward_sites.cpp's own guard block moved (a LoRA-arc merge, a RmsNormSite signature
// change, and later helper functions inserted above RunLayerLoopImpl all shifted it) --
// never by applying a line-count offset or eye-matching old numbers. Each time, the
// STATUS SET half of tests/ci/check_gpu_guard_status_parity.py (which reads the function's
// real body rather than these citations) reddened on the true old/new mismatch and is what
// caught the drift, and stayed green throughout once the citation set itself was unaffected
// by a given change -- confirming this file's own citations, not the guard set itself, are
// what a source-shape change actually moves.
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(LayerBudgetZero, InvalidLayerBudget, "forward_sites.cpp:1441")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(ContextCapNonPositive, InvalidContextCap, "forward_sites.cpp:1451")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(HeadDimGeometryMismatch, HeadDimGeometryMismatch, "forward_sites.cpp:1467-1468")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(KvHeadGeometryMismatch, KvHeadGeometryMismatch, "forward_sites.cpp:1477-1479")
// Guard 5 is one CPU check with two distinct rejecting outcomes: an
// overflow anywhere in the factor-by-factor KV-size product returns
// InvalidContextCap; a null or undersized workspace (checked only once the
// product itself is known not to have overflowed) returns WorkspaceTooSmall.
// Table entries stay one row per source guard, matching the reconfirmation
// review's own 9-row enumeration exactly, so `kCount` counts what a CPU
// reader counts, not what a naive per-`return`-statement scan would.
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(WorkspaceSizeOrOverflow, WorkspaceTooSmall, "forward_sites.cpp:1500-1510 (InvalidContextCap on overflow, WorkspaceTooSmall on null/undersized)")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(HiddenCodesNull, InvalidHiddenCodes, "forward_sites.cpp:1602")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(SequenceAlreadyComplete, SequenceAlreadyComplete, "forward_sites.cpp:1620")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(PositionOverCap, PositionOverCap, "forward_sites.cpp:1655")
SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD(KvCapacityExhausted, KvCapacityExhausted, "forward_sites.cpp:1656-1657")

#undef SSLM_GPU_LAYER_LOOP_GUARD
